1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,064
Hey, I'm Kalvin.

2
00:00:01,064 --> 00:00:03,193
I put this project together to

3
00:00:03,193 --> 00:00:04,967
explore a basic hospital question:

4
00:00:04,967 --> 00:00:07,096
which bed is available, and when

5
00:00:07,096 --> 00:00:09,224
might the next one be ready?

6
00:00:09,224 --> 00:00:11,353
It is a small prototype with

7
00:00:11,353 --> 00:00:13,482
fictional patients. I wanted to make

8
00:00:13,482 --> 00:00:15,610
something others could run, change, and

9
00:00:15,610 --> 00:00:16,320
learn from.

10
00:00:16,958 --> 00:00:18,935
The files live on GitHub.

11
00:00:18,935 --> 00:00:21,308
With Docker Desktop running, clone the

12
00:00:21,308 --> 00:00:23,680
repository, move into the project folder,

13
00:00:23,680 --> 00:00:26,052
and run the Compose command shown

14
00:00:26,052 --> 00:00:28,424
here. Open localhost, port eighty-seven eighty-seven.

15
00:00:28,424 --> 00:00:30,006
Leave the terminal running.

16
00:00:30,006 --> 00:00:31,983
The first build needs internet.

17
00:00:31,983 --> 00:00:33,564
Docker packages the app;

18
00:00:33,564 --> 00:00:35,937
it does not include Jev's model

19
00:00:35,937 --> 00:00:37,518
or an API key.

20
00:00:38,125 --> 00:00:40,611
The board shows twenty made-up beds

21
00:00:40,611 --> 00:00:43,098
across maternity, gynecology, and day surgery.

22
00:00:43,098 --> 00:00:45,584
There are three available, twelve occupied,

23
00:00:45,584 --> 00:00:48,071
three in turnaround, and two on

24
00:00:48,071 --> 00:00:50,557
hold. Read each status with its

25
00:00:50,557 --> 00:00:53,043
estimated time. An unknown estimate stays

26
00:00:53,043 --> 00:00:55,530
unknown. This board uses a fixed

27
00:00:55,530 --> 00:00:58,016
demo clock. A countdown never releases

28
00:00:58,016 --> 00:00:58,845
a bed.

29
00:00:59,458 --> 00:01:01,595
For a nurse, start by selecting

30
00:01:01,595 --> 00:01:03,731
a bed. Here we have MAT

31
00:01:03,731 --> 00:01:05,868
zero two, a fictional cesarean patient,

32
00:01:05,868 --> 00:01:08,004
pain rated four out of five,

33
00:01:08,004 --> 00:01:10,141
and four of eight milestones met.

34
00:01:10,141 --> 00:01:12,277
The progress note should say what

35
00:01:12,277 --> 00:01:14,414
changed and which follow-up is pending.

36
00:01:14,414 --> 00:01:16,550
These examples do not replace a

37
00:01:16,550 --> 00:01:17,618
care team's assessment.

38
00:01:18,250 --> 00:01:20,181
Choose the Improving practice note.

39
00:01:20,181 --> 00:01:22,498
It explicitly says walking and meals

40
00:01:22,498 --> 00:01:24,042
are better than yesterday.

41
00:01:24,042 --> 00:01:25,587
Press Show expected answer.

42
00:01:25,587 --> 00:01:27,904
The result is marked sample only.

43
00:01:27,904 --> 00:01:30,221
This is a written example answer,

44
00:01:30,221 --> 00:01:31,765
with no model call.

45
00:01:31,765 --> 00:01:34,082
It describes improvement in the note,

46
00:01:34,082 --> 00:01:36,399
but it does not confirm discharge

47
00:01:36,399 --> 00:01:38,330
or change the bed's status.

48
00:01:38,958 --> 00:01:40,502
Now choose Needs review.

49
00:01:40,502 --> 00:01:42,817
Pain is limiting walking, nursing reassessment

50
00:01:42,817 --> 00:01:45,132
is pending, and departure is delayed.

51
00:01:45,132 --> 00:01:46,676
Those are explicit blockers.

52
00:01:46,676 --> 00:01:48,991
The written expected answer is needs

53
00:01:48,991 --> 00:01:51,307
review. That means the observations and

54
00:01:51,307 --> 00:01:52,464
follow-up need attention.

55
00:01:52,464 --> 00:01:54,780
It is not an instruction to

56
00:01:54,780 --> 00:01:57,095
discharge the patient or assign the

57
00:01:57,095 --> 00:01:58,638
bed to someone else.

58
00:01:59,250 --> 00:02:01,415
The Unclear case says an update

59
00:02:01,415 --> 00:02:03,579
was received, but it gives no

60
00:02:03,579 --> 00:02:05,744
recovery trend. Its written expected category

61
00:02:05,744 --> 00:02:07,908
is unclear. For a live unclear

62
00:02:07,908 --> 00:02:10,073
answer, the app asks for more

63
00:02:10,073 --> 00:02:12,237
detail about changes in pain, walking

64
00:02:12,237 --> 00:02:14,041
or meals, and pending reassessment.

65
00:02:14,041 --> 00:02:16,205
That is feedback on the note,

66
00:02:16,205 --> 00:02:18,370
not a grade for the nurse.

67
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:21,212
If I edit the note, the

68
00:02:21,212 --> 00:02:23,424
old answer clears and the practice

69
00:02:23,424 --> 00:02:25,637
selection resets. The app says to

70
00:02:25,637 --> 00:02:27,849
use an unchanged practice case or

71
00:02:27,849 --> 00:02:28,955
ask Jev again.

72
00:02:28,955 --> 00:02:31,167
That keeps an answer from quietly

73
00:02:31,167 --> 00:02:32,642
following a different note.

74
00:02:32,642 --> 00:02:34,854
Changing the selected bed also clears

75
00:02:34,854 --> 00:02:35,960
the old result.

76
00:02:36,583 --> 00:02:38,609
No key was needed for those

77
00:02:38,609 --> 00:02:40,635
practice cases. Open Jev connection and

78
00:02:40,635 --> 00:02:42,323
setup, then check server setup.

79
00:02:42,323 --> 00:02:44,349
This copy has no server key.

80
00:02:44,349 --> 00:02:46,375
Someone can supply their own key

81
00:02:46,375 --> 00:02:48,400
in the optional field or through

82
00:02:48,400 --> 00:02:49,413
the server environment.

83
00:02:49,413 --> 00:02:51,439
Ask Jev is the button that

84
00:02:51,439 --> 00:02:53,465
sends the fictional note to TypeSafe.

85
00:02:53,465 --> 00:02:55,490
We are not making that call

86
00:02:55,490 --> 00:02:56,503
in this walkthrough.

87
00:02:57,125 --> 00:02:59,525
Choose Time room cleaning and select

88
00:02:59,525 --> 00:03:00,725
a turnaround room.

89
00:03:00,725 --> 00:03:03,125
Start cleaning records the computer's actual

90
00:03:03,125 --> 00:03:05,525
time. The timer saves room IDs

91
00:03:05,525 --> 00:03:07,925
and timestamps in this browser, and

92
00:03:07,925 --> 00:03:09,525
resumes after a reload.

93
00:03:09,525 --> 00:03:11,925
Finish cleaning records elapsed minutes and

94
00:03:11,925 --> 00:03:13,525
updates the local average.

95
00:03:13,525 --> 00:03:15,925
It still says awaiting staff release.

96
00:03:15,925 --> 00:03:18,325
These few seconds only demonstrate the

97
00:03:18,325 --> 00:03:20,725
timer; they are not a typical

98
00:03:20,725 --> 00:03:21,525
cleaning duration.

99
00:03:22,125 --> 00:03:24,276
There is also a separate readiness

100
00:03:24,276 --> 00:03:26,428
example you can run from the

101
00:03:26,428 --> 00:03:28,579
command line. It considers departure windows,

102
00:03:28,579 --> 00:03:30,731
cleaner availability, breaks, and a shift.

103
00:03:30,731 --> 00:03:32,882
The two-room example shows estimated windows

104
00:03:32,882 --> 00:03:34,316
in New York time.

105
00:03:34,316 --> 00:03:36,468
Both rooms remain occupied, with release

106
00:03:36,468 --> 00:03:38,619
pending. This timing function is not

107
00:03:38,619 --> 00:03:40,771
connected to the board yet and

108
00:03:40,771 --> 00:03:42,205
needs no Jev key.

109
00:03:42,833 --> 00:03:44,977
The separate data pipeline can replay

110
00:03:44,977 --> 00:03:47,120
saved public aggregate counts from New

111
00:03:47,120 --> 00:03:49,264
York for twenty twenty-three and twenty

112
00:03:49,264 --> 00:03:51,408
twenty-four, offline. Those stay lengths cover

113
00:03:51,408 --> 00:03:52,479
the whole admission;

114
00:03:52,479 --> 00:03:54,623
they are not individual bed-release forecasts.

115
00:03:54,623 --> 00:03:56,766
The project has five hundred and

116
00:03:56,766 --> 00:03:57,838
two automated tests.

117
00:03:57,838 --> 00:03:59,982
GitHub also checks Docker build exclusions.

118
00:03:59,982 --> 00:04:01,411
It is a gift:

119
00:04:01,411 --> 00:04:03,554
try it, change it, or build

120
00:04:03,554 --> 00:04:05,698
on it. Keep it to fictional

121
00:04:05,698 --> 00:04:07,842
patients. This is a demo for

122
00:04:07,842 --> 00:04:08,913
learning and review.
